# Final expense state-page copy. Every figure is from the verified 2026
# price-list analysis in docs/content/state-pages.md (state medians; national
# medians $1,995 cremation / $2,800 immediate burial / $2,195 basic services
# fee / $6,415 full traditional funeral; cemetery $2,000-$5,000). Regulator
# details are from each department's homepage, fetched 12 Sep 2026.
P = lambda *ps: "\n".join(f"<p>{p}</p>" for p in ps)
COPY = {}
# Recommended coverage band per state, rendered in the coverage callout. Every coverage note below
# concludes at the same two figures: cremation with a gathering within $10,000, burial with a
# service toward $15,000. Override per slug if a state's note ever concludes differently.
BAND = {slug: (10000, 15000) for slug in ("colorado", "tennessee", "florida", "texas", "california", "pennsylvania", "ohio", "michigan", "north-carolina", "georgia", "arizona")}

COPY["colorado"] = dict(
 intro=P("A direct cremation in Colorado has a median cost of $1,650 and an immediate burial $2,565 &mdash; 17% and 8% below the national medians, and cheaper than average on every funeral-home charge we measured. Final expense insurance covers those costs with a small whole life policy, usually $5,000 to $25,000, that pays cash directly to your family.",
         "This is our home state. Senior Solutions Insurance works from Greenwood Village, and the licensed agent who calls you back sits in that office."),
 obs="<p><strong>Colorado is cheaper than average on every measure.</strong> Cremation at $1,650 is 17% below the national median and burial at $2,565 is 8% below; the non-declinable basic services fee, $1,985, is 10% below. Colorado families generally need less coverage for the funeral home&rsquo;s bill than the national averages suggest &mdash; the cemetery is where the number climbs.</p>",
 cov="<p>For a Colorado family choosing burial, the funeral home&rsquo;s bill is below average but the cemetery&rsquo;s is not, so the Front Range plot price tends to decide the total. Ten thousand dollars covers the simplest burial with room to spare; a full service with a viewing points toward $10,000 to $15,000.</p>",
 reg="<p>Final expense policies sold in Colorado are regulated by the <a href=\"https://doi.colorado.gov/\" rel=\"noopener\">Colorado Division of Insurance</a>, which licenses the agents and carriers, takes complaints, and offers a &ldquo;Find Lost Life Insurance&rdquo; search on its homepage for families who believe a deceased relative had a policy they cannot locate &mdash; worth knowing before you buy, and worth telling your family about after. Senior Solutions Insurance is licensed there, in our own state; you can verify our agents on the same site.</p>",
 faq=[
  ("How much does a funeral cost in Colorado?", "The 2026 median from 31 Colorado funeral home price lists is $1,650 for a direct cremation and $2,565 for an immediate burial, both below the national medians of $1,995 and $2,800. Those are funeral-home charges only; the cemetery bills a plot, vault, opening and closing, and headstone separately, typically $2,000&ndash;$5,000 or more."),
  ("Is final expense insurance cheaper in Colorado?", "No. Premiums are set by your age, gender, tobacco use and health, and a 65-year-old in Denver is quoted from the same tables as a 65-year-old in Atlanta. What differs in Colorado is what you are insuring against: funeral-home charges here run 8 to 17% below national, so the same $10,000 policy stretches further."),
  ("How much coverage do I need for a Colorado funeral?", "Add the cemetery to the funeral home. An immediate burial at Colorado&rsquo;s $2,565 median plus $2,000&ndash;$5,000 of cemetery costs is $4,565&ndash;$7,565; a full traditional funeral with a viewing lands at $8,415&ndash;$11,415. Most Colorado families land between $10,000 and $15,000 once medical bills and small debts are added."),
  ("What if my family cannot find the policy after I die?", "Colorado is one of the states whose insurance regulator runs a lost-policy search: the Colorado Division of Insurance&rsquo;s &ldquo;Find Lost Life Insurance&rdquo; service, on its homepage, asks participating insurers to check their records for a deceased person. It is a backstop, not a plan &mdash; tell the person you name as beneficiary where the policy is and who the agent was."),
  ("Where is Senior Solutions Insurance located?", "In Greenwood Village, Colorado, at 5775 DTC Blvd, Suite 250-S. The agent who calls you back works from that office and is licensed by the Colorado Division of Insurance, where you can verify the license."),
 ],
)

COPY["tennessee"] = dict(
 intro=P("A direct cremation in Tennessee has a median cost of $2,348 &mdash; 18% above the national median of $1,995 &mdash; and an immediate burial $2,990, which is also above the national figure. Final expense insurance covers those costs with a small whole life policy, usually $5,000 to $25,000, that pays cash directly to your family."),
 obs="<p><strong>Tennessee runs above the national median on every funeral-home charge.</strong> Cremation at $2,348 is 18% above national, burial at $2,990 is 7% above, and the non-declinable basic services fee of $2,298 is 5% above. If you assumed a Tennessee funeral would be cheaper than average, the price lists say otherwise; plan the coverage amount accordingly.</p>",
 cov="<p>Because Tennessee&rsquo;s funeral-home charges sit above national, a Tennessee family should not round down. An immediate burial with cemetery costs already approaches $8,000 at the top of the range; a full service with a viewing points toward $15,000 rather than $10,000.</p>",
 reg="<p>Final expense policies sold in Tennessee are regulated by the Insurance Division of the <a href=\"https://www.tn.gov/commerce/insurance-division.html\" rel=\"noopener\">Tennessee Department of Commerce and Insurance</a>, whose site lets you verify an agent&rsquo;s license and file a complaint. Ask your agent for their Tennessee license number and check it before you apply.</p>",
 faq=[
  ("How much does a funeral cost in Tennessee?", "From 26 Tennessee funeral home price lists collected in 2026, the median is $2,348 for a direct cremation and $2,990 for an immediate burial. Both are above the national medians of $1,995 and $2,800. Cemetery costs &mdash; plot, vault, opening and closing, headstone &mdash; are billed separately and add $2,000&ndash;$5,000 or more."),
  ("Why is cremation more expensive in Tennessee than the national average?", "The price lists do not say why, only that it is: Tennessee&rsquo;s $2,348 median for direct cremation is 18% above the national $1,995, and the basic services fee of $2,298 is above national too. The practical point is not to size a Tennessee policy on the national average."),
  ("How much final expense coverage does a Tennessee family need?", "An immediate burial at Tennessee&rsquo;s $2,990 median plus $2,000&ndash;$5,000 of cemetery costs is $4,990&ndash;$7,990 before any service. A full traditional funeral with a viewing runs $8,415&ndash;$11,415 with the cemetery included. With medical bills and small debts added, $10,000 to $15,000 is where most Tennessee families land, and the higher figure is the safer one here."),
  ("Does Tennessee&rsquo;s higher funeral cost change my premium?", "No &mdash; it changes the amount you should buy. Premiums come from the same age, gender, tobacco and health tables everywhere, but Tennessee&rsquo;s $2,298 basic services fee and $2,990 burial median mean a $10,000 policy covers a smaller share of a Tennessee funeral than it would in Arizona or Colorado. Most Tennessee families who want a burial with a service should be looking at $15,000."),
  ("Who regulates final expense insurance in Tennessee?", "The Insurance Division of the Tennessee Department of Commerce and Insurance, which licenses the agents and carriers and whose website lets you verify a license and file a complaint. The same department houses the TennCare Oversight Division, so a Tennessee family sorting out a relative&rsquo;s Medicaid and a funeral policy at the same time is dealing with one agency. Ask your agent for their Tennessee license number."),
 ],
)

COPY["florida"] = dict(
 intro=P("A direct cremation in Florida has a median cost of $1,882 and an immediate burial $2,695 &mdash; within a few percent of the national medians on every funeral-home charge. What moves the total for a Florida family is not the funeral home; it is the cemetery, which is left out of every published average. Final expense insurance covers both with a small whole life policy, usually $5,000 to $25,000, that pays cash directly to your family."),
 obs="<p><strong>Florida tracks the national median closely</strong> &mdash; cremation 6% below, burial 4% below, the basic services fee 8% below &mdash; so the national averages are a fair guide to the funeral home&rsquo;s bill here. The figure they leave out is the cemetery: a plot, vault, opening and closing and a marker add $2,000&ndash;$5,000 or more, and that line, not the funeral home&rsquo;s, is what moves a Florida total.</p>",
 cov="<p>With the funeral home near the national average, Florida&rsquo;s coverage decision comes down to the cemetery and to whether the family wants a viewing. Cremation with a memorial gathering can be covered comfortably by $10,000; burial with a service in a metro-area cemetery points toward $15,000.</p>",
 reg="<p>Final expense policies sold in Florida are regulated through the Florida Department of Financial Services, whose <a href=\"https://www.myfloridacfo.com/division/consumers\" rel=\"noopener\">Division of Consumer Services</a> runs the consumer helpline and takes complaints about agents and insurers. Unusually, the same department also regulates Florida&rsquo;s funeral homes and cemeteries through its Division of Funeral, Cemetery and Consumer Services &mdash; so the agency that licenses your insurance agent is also the one that oversees the funeral home&rsquo;s price list.</p>",
 faq=[
  ("How much does a funeral cost in Florida?", "From 32 Florida funeral home price lists collected in 2026, the median direct cremation is $1,882 and the median immediate burial $2,695 &mdash; within a few percent of the national medians of $1,995 and $2,800. Those are funeral-home charges only; cemetery costs add $2,000&ndash;$5,000 or more on top."),
  ("Why do Florida funeral costs vary so much between quotes?", "Because the cemetery is a separate business with a separate bill. Florida&rsquo;s funeral-home medians are steady and close to national &mdash; $1,882 for cremation against $1,995, $2,695 for burial against $2,800 &mdash; so the spread you see between Florida quotes is mostly the cemetery, the casket and whether a viewing is included. The Florida Department of Financial Services regulates both the funeral homes and the cemeteries, and the FTC Funeral Rule entitles you to an itemized price list from any of them."),
  ("How much final expense coverage does a Florida family need?", "An immediate burial at Florida&rsquo;s $2,695 median plus $2,000&ndash;$5,000 of cemetery costs is $4,695&ndash;$7,695. A full traditional funeral with a viewing and cemetery costs lands at $8,415&ndash;$11,415. Add medical bills and small debts and most Florida families choose between $10,000 and $15,000."),
  ("Who do I complain to about a Florida funeral home or an insurance agent?", "The same department, as it happens. The Florida Department of Financial Services licenses insurance agents through its Division of Consumer Services and regulates funeral homes and cemeteries through its Division of Funeral, Cemetery and Consumer Services. Its consumer helpline handles both; the FTC Funeral Rule also gives you the right to an itemized price list from any Florida funeral home."),
  ("Can I buy final expense insurance in Florida if I spend part of the year elsewhere?", "Yes. A final expense policy is not tied to a funeral home or a state; it pays cash to the person you name, wherever the funeral takes place. If that ends up being Florida, the Florida medians &mdash; $1,882 for cremation, $2,695 for burial, plus the cemetery &mdash; are the right benchmark for the amount; if it happens in your other state, that state&rsquo;s figures apply and the cash benefit is the same. Tell the agent which state is your legal residence, because a Florida-issued policy falls under the Florida Department of Financial Services&rsquo; rules."),
 ],
)

COPY["texas"] = dict(
 intro=P("A direct cremation in Texas has a median cost of $2,015, right at the national median of $1,995, while an immediate burial at $2,890 sits above the national $2,800. Final expense insurance covers those costs with a small whole life policy, usually $5,000 to $25,000, that pays cash directly to your family."),
 obs="<p><strong>Texas splits the difference:</strong> cremation is 1% above national, essentially at it, while burial is 3% above and the non-declinable basic services fee of $2,245 is 2% above. In a state this size the medians hide a wide spread between the big metros and the rest, and the 40 price lists behind these figures make Texas one of the better-sampled states in the set.</p>",
 cov="<p>Texas burial costs run a little above national, and a metro cemetery in Dallas&ndash;Fort Worth, Houston or Austin is more likely to sit toward the top of the $2,000&ndash;$5,000 range than a rural one. A Texas family planning a burial with a service should size toward $15,000; cremation with a memorial is comfortably within $10,000.</p>",
 reg="<p>Final expense policies sold in Texas are regulated by the <a href=\"https://www.tdi.texas.gov/\" rel=\"noopener\">Texas Department of Insurance</a>, whose homepage lets you look up a company or agent and file a complaint. Texas also has a separate Office of Public Insurance Counsel that represents consumers in insurance matters, linked from the department&rsquo;s site. Ask your agent for their Texas license number.</p>",
 faq=[
  ("How much does a funeral cost in Texas?", "From 40 Texas funeral home price lists collected in 2026, the median direct cremation is $2,015 and the median immediate burial $2,890. Cremation is at the national median of $1,995; burial is above the national $2,800. Cemetery costs are separate and add $2,000&ndash;$5,000 or more."),
  ("Is burial or cremation the bigger saving in Texas?", "Cremation. The gap between Texas&rsquo;s $2,015 cremation and $2,890 burial medians is about 43% before the cemetery is counted, and choosing cremation also removes the plot, vault and marker. For a Texas family the choice between the two changes the coverage need by several thousand dollars."),
  ("How much final expense coverage does a Texas family need?", "An immediate burial at Texas&rsquo;s $2,890 median plus $2,000&ndash;$5,000 of cemetery costs is $4,890&ndash;$7,890. A full traditional funeral with a viewing and cemetery costs comes to $8,415&ndash;$11,415. With medical bills and small debts added, most Texas families land between $10,000 and $15,000."),
  ("Do Texas funeral costs differ between Houston and a small town?", "The medians above blend both, and the spread inside Texas is wide: 40 price lists across 254 counties is a large sample precisely because the state is. The basic services fee, $2,245 at the median, is the steadiest charge; the cemetery plot is where a Houston, Dallas or Austin funeral pulls away from a rural one. Ask the funeral home for its General Price List &mdash; Texas homes must provide it &mdash; and compare that fee first."),
  ("Who regulates final expense insurance in Texas?", "The Texas Department of Insurance licenses the agents and carriers; its homepage has an agent and company lookup and a complaint form. If a dispute goes further, Texas&rsquo;s Office of Public Insurance Counsel represents consumers in insurance matters."),
 ],
)

